FAILURE MODE 16 (proposed BUILD-DISCIPLINE pattern), from /ideate 2026-07-28 human-directed deep-dive. NAME: 'THE BLOCKER THAT NAMES THE WRONG LAYER'. SHAPE: an agent is genuinely blocked, correctly recognizes it, and correctly escalates to the human -- but it can only observe its own refusal, not the cause of that refusal, so it escalates an UNDER-SPECIFIED blocker naming the wrong layer of a stack. The human then fixes the named layer, the block survives, and the incident reads afterwards as if the fix did not work. LIVE INSTANCE, verified tonight by probe not by quote: aedile's 2026-07-27 svc-vaporwave run reported 'this cycle's own permission classifier blocked reading the .aedile-api-secrets file', filed it in QUESTIONS.md, and its report says the next cycle 'still needs a human decision on the credential-access blocker'. That blocker is currently queued to spend Zach's attention. But the unix layer is NOT the blocker: /srv/vaporwave-reports/aedile/.aedile-api-secrets is mode 0640 root:vaporwave-reports, svc-vaporwave IS a member of vaporwave-reports (id svc-vaporwave -> groups=1001(svc-vaporwave),1002(vaporwave-reports)), and 'sudo -u svc-vaporwave [ -r <file> ]' returns TRUE -- the account can read the file today, no change needed. The real block is the Claude tool-permission classifier inside the run. The obvious human reading of the escalation -- loosen the file permissions -- would change nothing and the blocker would return identically tomorrow night. COST PROFILE: identical to pattern 14 (fails toward alarm, alarm routed to the scarcest organ) but the mechanism is different and worth separating: pattern 14 is a sensor reporting a negative it never checked. Here the sensor is HONEST -- the agent really was blocked, and said so accurately -- and it still misleads, because 'I was refused' and 'what refused me' are different facts and only the first is observable from inside the refusal. An honest sensor with no visibility into its own stack is a new shape. CHECKABLE FORM: any blocker escalated to a human that concerns access to a named filesystem path must carry a machine-probed witness of the layers it is NOT -- at minimum 'unix: readable/not readable by <account>, verified <date> via <command>'. That is exactly CLAUDE.md's existing stamp rule applied to blockers rather than to state claims, and scheduler's new questions-lint.sh already implements the stamp check for QUESTIONS.md -- extending it to BLOCKERS.md entries asserting a permission fact is the smallest wiring that would have caught this one.
